{
  "about": "A Deployment's, StatefulSet's or DaemonSet's verdict once its pods are asked, as both halves read it: `with_starts` in src/tauri/src/resources/workloads/rollout.rs (the page, the peek, Share, Needs attention, the census and Connections) and `withStarts` in src/ui/lib/workload-status.ts (the lists). Neither set kind writes a condition that tells a pod still starting from one that never will, and a Deployment's scale leaves Progressing at NewReplicaSetAvailable, so a workload short of available pods is coming up only while some pod of its own is still starting and none shows a fault. A Deployment's own pods are those its ReplicaSets run. Each pod arrives as `pod_start` read it in Rust: settled (ready, finished or terminating), starting until an instant, or failing. A start whose instant has passed is the fault. No pod starting means nothing explains the gap: a pod the controller could not create looks exactly like that, so the workload stays as it read. The same holds for a workload with none available yet (unavailable at zero) and for one whose newest spec its controller has not read (unobserved), each carrying its counts: a pod already starting is the evidence the counts lag. Unavailable with some still available, and every other state, passes through. Pods that could not be read are null, not an empty list: a verdict they could have changed becomes podsUnread, the controller's verdict alone, and any other passes through.",
